{"authored" [{"question" "q-95114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $y={x}^{3},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nAssume $x>0$." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-95114/review.json" "file_sha256" "b4d417977480499697a7fb24668d83aacc9c5ff382bc51d82b98063ac1abdba7" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-95114/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "b1caa3c6a9452d2ca0c6092217e7f671cdbf9295e01c0082c4522f1f2689c9ee" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-95114/verification.json" "basis" 2342 "rationale" "The author solution replaces sqrt(x²) by x, which is valid for positive x. Minimal explicit x>0 premise makes the original author key and all source derivation valid. Retain original worked solution and append the independently proved signed derivative and origin note; all source choices, answer identities and fields remain unchanged."} {"question" "q-95114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{4})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{4})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}+\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDomain clarification: The problem assumes $x>0$, so $\\sqrt{x^2}=x$ in the source derivation above. Without that assumption, $f(x,x^3)=|x|\\sqrt{1+x^4}$ and\n$$\\frac{df}{dx}=\\operatorname{sgn}(x)\\frac{1+3x^4}{\\sqrt{1+x^4}}\\quad(x\\ne0).$$\nFor $x<0$ the derivative is the negative of the displayed answer. At $x=0$, the one-sided difference quotients tend to $-1$ and $1$, so the derivative does not exist. The original unqualified prompt and worked solution remain preserved in the source capture and database history." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-95114/review.json" "file_sha256" "b4d417977480499697a7fb24668d83aacc9c5ff382bc51d82b98063ac1abdba7" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-95114/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "b1caa3c6a9452d2ca0c6092217e7f671cdbf9295e01c0082c4522f1f2689c9ee" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-95114/verification.json" "basis" 2342 "rationale" "The author solution replaces sqrt(x²) by x, which is valid for positive x. Minimal explicit x>0 premise makes the original author key and all source derivation valid. Retain original worked solution and append the independently proved signed derivative and origin note; all source choices, answer identities and fields remain unchanged."} {"question" "q-95114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1+3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-95114/review.json" "file_sha256" "b4d417977480499697a7fb24668d83aacc9c5ff382bc51d82b98063ac1abdba7" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-95114/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "b1caa3c6a9452d2ca0c6092217e7f671cdbf9295e01c0082c4522f1f2689c9ee" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-95114/verification.json" "basis" 2342 "rationale" "The author solution replaces sqrt(x²) by x, which is valid for positive x. Minimal explicit x>0 premise makes the original author key and all source derivation valid. Retain original worked solution and append the independently proved signed derivative and origin note; all source choices, answer identities and fields remain unchanged."} {"question" "q-95114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $y={x}^{3},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069068/state.json" "file_sha256" "777dfcf01b96a787b28b68f81fb3530e5914d36ace60e3f720fc76b05d4152b4" "basis" 2341 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069068/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-95114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{4})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{4})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}+\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069068/state.json" "file_sha256" "777dfcf01b96a787b28b68f81fb3530e5914d36ace60e3f720fc76b05d4152b4" "basis" 2341 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069068/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-95114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069068/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3d9983219d7503fb86a3ac5df0b17f8e61a10ebb675724aefba39c30056ec6ab" "b